From b31f599f3e524968bdd0423ac88ce02b3fc0715f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FiyeroT <me@fiyero.xyz>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 11:24:11 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 62/63] warm: the deep paths of a new UDP session and of its
 DNS exchange on goroutines kept warm

A new goroutine starts with a small stack, and the runtime keeps no grown stack for the next one:
a path deeper than that stack copies the stack larger every time it runs on a new goroutine. Every
DNS query of a resolver such as dnsmasq comes from a port of its own, a new UDP session: it ran the
route of the session on the new goroutine udpnat starts for it, its exchange on another new one
(dns_nat.go), the query of plain DNS from a socket of its own on a third (dns/udp), and the A
question an AAAA one asks first under ipv6_if_no_ipv4 on yet another. At DNS saturation the
copying (runtime.newstack, runtime.copystack) took a third of the CPU on arm64 (16 % with 1.13,
whose path is shallower).

common/warm keeps the goroutines that ran a function for a while (10 s, as long as a DNS session
waits for its next packet) and hands the next function to one that waits, its grown stack kept;
with none waiting a function gets a new goroutine, so no caller ever waits. The direct inbound
routes a new session on one, the DNS NAT handler exchanges on one, plain DNS over UDP sends a
query from its own socket on one, and the DNS client asks the A question of ipv6_if_no_ipv4 on
one. On an arm64 server, 5 rounds an arm, Mann-Whitney p <= 0.016: at DNS saturation 32 % more
queries a second and 24 % less CPU a query (beyond 1.13: +10 %, -9 %), copystack 19 % -> 0.5 % of
the CPU; at 500 queries a second of real names, the last two of the four 3-5 % less CPU a query
(A and AAAA, a new port a query or 32 ports); at most 8 workers, no more memory, none left 15 s
after the load.

Pdk-Feature: warm-goroutines
Pdk-Zone: core
Pdk-Layer: 05
